iec104 — provenance note
========================

A record only. Nothing in this file imposes a redistribution condition beyond
zig-libs' own MIT license (see the repository `LICENSE`). The one third party
named below is strongly copyleft; it is named with its licence precisely
because the argument for why nothing is owed has to be checkable.

Original work
-------------

The module is this repository's own implementation of IEC 60870-5-104, a
public IEC standard.

Test oracle: c104 (GPL-3.0-or-later)
------------------------------------

`src/goldens.zig` freezes wire traffic between a c104 2.2.1 controlling station
and a c104 controlled station (Martin Unkel, Fraunhofer FIT; a pybind11 binding
over lib60870-C, both **GPL-3.0**), recorded through a TCP proxy. The scripts
that took those captures and drive this module's outstation with a real c104
master live in `tools/` (`global_ca_capture.py`, `global_ca_capture2.py`,
`live_global_ca_master.py`). They are this repository's own files (MIT).

c104 is used as a black box only. It is installed in a throwaway virtualenv by
whoever runs those scripts; it is never vendored, never a build dependency,
never linked into anything this repository builds, and no committed test
imports or runs it — `zig build test-iec104` replays the frozen bytes. No part
of c104 or lib60870-C — source, header, binary or documentation text — is
copied, translated or redistributed here, and its source was not read. What is
committed is the octet stream of a conversation between separate processes
speaking a public standard, plus c104's own textual decode of those octets.
The GPL attaches to c104's own work, and none of c104's own work is here.

This is the same posture, and the same reasoning, as
`modules/fleetsim/NOTICE` for its c104 master
(`scripts/vm/guests/fleetsim-iec104-master.py`).
